From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zhenyao Mo <zmo@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:09:48 -0700
Subject: gpu: Don't mark attachments cleared when the lazy clear fails

GLES2DecoderImpl::ClearUnclearedAttachments() performs the
security-critical lazy clear of never-written framebuffer attachments
and then unconditionally calls MarkAttachmentsAsCleared(), without
checking whether the driver clear actually executed.

GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY may be generated by any command (OpenGL ES 3.2 section
2.3.1) and leaves the results of that command undefined. Drivers that
defer physical allocation until first use can therefore fail the clear
under VRAM pressure. When that happens the attachment still holds
whatever a previous consumer left in that GPU memory, but it is
bookkept as cleared, so a subsequent ReadPixels() copies that memory
into the client's shared memory. GPU memory pools are shared across
origins, so the disclosed contents can be cross-origin.

The failure is not otherwise observable by the decoder:

 - The default path calls api()->glClearFn() and never peeks, so the
   queued error is left on the driver and is later drained by an
   unrelated glGetError(). ErrorStateImpl::GetGLError() returns the raw
   driver error without routing it through SetGLError(), so
   OnOutOfMemoryError() never fires and the context is not lost.
 - The gl_clear_broken workaround path runs the blit inside a
   ScopedGLErrorSuppressor, whose destructor silently discards
   GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY and GL_CONTEXT_LOST_KHR.
 - Framebuffer::ClearUnclearedIntRenderbufferAttachments() has the same
   problem for integer renderbuffers: it calls SetCleared(true) after
   glClearBuffer{ui,i}v() without checking for an error.

Fix these sites so that "cleared" bookkeeping is only committed once
the clear is known to have landed:

 - Bracket the glClear() with the decoder's established
   COPY_REAL_GL_ERRORS / PEEK_GL_ERROR pattern, already used by
   ClearLevel3D(), ClearCompressedTextureLevel() and
   DoRenderbufferStorage(). Peeking also routes GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY
   through OnOutOfMemoryError() so lose_context_when_out_of_memory_
   applies.
 - Make ClearFramebufferForWorkaround() peek inside the suppressor's
   scope and return whether the blit succeeded. The suppressor is kept
   because the function has two other callers that must not leak blit
   errors into unrelated commands. Surfacing the first error to those
   callers is bounded, because ClearRealGLErrors() already NOTREACHED()s
   on anything other than GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY and GL_CONTEXT_LOST_KHR.
 - Make ClearUnclearedIntRenderbufferAttachments() return the first
   error it saw and only SetCleared() the attachments whose clear
   succeeded. The decoder re-raises that error through the wrapper, so
   the real error code is preserved rather than being reported as a
   blanket GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY. Its result is [[nodiscard]] so the check
   cannot be silently dropped later.
 - Also report failure for integer attachments beyond max_draw_buffers.
   Those cannot be addressed by glClearBuffer*iv at all, so they were
   skipped without being cleared and then marked cleared by the caller.

When any clear fails the function now returns false without marking
anything cleared, which CheckFramebufferValid() already propagates, so
the caller reports GL_INVALID_FRAMEBUFFER_OPERATION and the read is
abandoned. The attachment stays uncleared and a later access retries
the clear.

Pre-existing driver errors are drained with CopyRealGLErrorsToWrapper()
rather than a bare glGetError() loop, so that errors from earlier
commands are still reported to the client and GL_CONTEXT_LOST_KHR still
reaches OnContextLostError().

GL state restoration (draw buffers, clear state, framebuffer rebind and
GL_RASTERIZER_DISCARD) is performed on both exit paths.

